WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
World Games, take on the world!

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:25.660
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.420
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.380
I'm Joe.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:30.440
And I'm Sam.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:42.820
Guys, I'm sure we're all familiar with sports like weightlifting and, you know, maybe a little familiar with cliff diving, but who really knew about the caber toss before today's episode?

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.960
My great uncle was a famous caber tosser.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.560
My great uncle was a famous caber.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.440
Yeah, my parents put me in Little League, Little League caber tossing, so...

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:05.060
You know what, we'll get into the games individually, but there's just, there's something funny about the caber toss one, because now I'm picturing, like, kids trying to do that same thing.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:06.660
And they would be awful at it.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:07.680
They would just suck.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.520
Well, how do you think they get trained?

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.940
I mean, how old do you think the people that caber toss are?

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:13.420
I mean, they gotta start somewhere.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:23.880
Okay, but like, you know how it's painful to watch, like, kids in chorus or in band, and you have to go to their, like, you know, local auditorium and watch them do their recitals or whatever?

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.000
Because you're, like, so inspired the whole time?

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.380
Yeah, exactly. And it would go through your tears?

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.140
Exactly. And now you have to do that with caber toss.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:35.720
You have to be like, oh, alright, he's gonna pick up the log again and try to balance it while he walks and then toss it a little.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.300
I feel like that would be very fun to watch.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:40.300
I don't know about you guys.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:47.320
It would probably be tough, though, to see the kids get smushed into the ground when they drop the caber and it lands on their head three distinct times, like it does in the game.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.500
Yeah. It's a low, it's a high turnover.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.280
A lot of people lost their lives.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:55.640
Yeah. Oh, geez.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:03.740
Dark turn. World Games is made by Epix, who also made, if you guys will recall, Winter Games.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.060
The game that we absolutely hate and has that ice skating game that doesn't make any sense.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:10.960
Did you guys know that?

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:12.000
They fixed it in this one. I didn't.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.300
It's, you know, these sort of, like, these Olympic games.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:24.380
Olympic-ish games all feel a little similar, but this one felt more different than the others, if that makes any sense.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:27.180
So I did not think that this was the same company.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.760
Yeah, I didn't either, but I was comparing them a lot.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:31.740
I think I was comparing them to Winter Games.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:38.860
Like you said, Sean, they kind of blur together for me, too, but I was comparing them because I remember really disliking some of those other ones.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.700
This one, I think, you know, there are some instances of improvements in this one compared to some old ones.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:52.620
Yeah, I think especially the, I mean, we're going to get into it, but I think especially the ice skating felt familiar to me.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.840
Like the barrel jump.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:58.920
Right. You're on skates in that one.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:09.920
Yeah. And so, you know, Epix released Summer Games first, and as you could imagine, that was a bunch of summer-themed sports that you would see in the Olympics and so on.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.820
And then Winter Games came, and that was like the Winter Olympics.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.620
They just couldn't use the name Olympics.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:25.700
I wonder what made them think like, you know, let's go with like a worldly motif and just grab like from different countries around the world a sport specific to them.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.260
Their most cartoonish sport.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:36.600
Right. Choose their most cartoonish sport in the sense of like characterizing the states, not the states, you know, like the United States there as like, you know, oh, they're cowboys.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:41.380
So do the bull riding, you know, it's like there's something funny about like Mexico and cliff diving.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.560
I don't know. Is that really like what they're famous for?

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:45.760
I have no idea.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.380
I'm just going to take their word for it.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.960
But I do like, I do like that.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.840
Is it Germany that you have to just jump over a bunch of barrels or something?

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.180
Yes, that's the barrel jumping.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:58.200
Yeah. That's, that's funny too.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:02.060
So, you know, but it's a good conceit though at the end of the day.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:10.680
Like I do have to give them credit that they followed through on it really well of like, well, if we're going to make it the world games and everything, and these are going to be kind of weird games that, you know, might not be familiar to everybody.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.800
It's going to make you more culturally aware.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:23.340
And then we're also going to give you these like splash screens before you go into each game that explain like, you know, a little bit about the, how the sport works and the history of it and stuff like that.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:30.460
I appreciated those insights because when I selected log rolling, I had no idea what I was going to be doing.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.640
Yeah. It gives you a little insight, like lets you immerse yourself a little more into it.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:38.560
It gives you a little history lesson on what you're playing and where it comes from.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:41.360
I think it's just a glorified, like we didn't make this up.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.120
I feel like a lot of people would think they did.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:52.500
That's fair. And there's something presentationally, like the way it's styled, this game makes me feel like I'm playing this one on a computer.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:53.480
Do you guys know what I mean?

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:57.660
It has like this like Apple II style graphics look to it.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:58.880
It feels like pitfall.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.480
Sure. That's a good, yeah, there you go.

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:06.160
Yeah. I think it's like the straight lines or something like the, the, it's like very like geometric.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.020
And that always reminds me of those little computer games.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:19.420
Even just that UI and the way that it like lays everything out, like the spacing of the menus and that the, like the options aren't terribly different from each other.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:26.240
But you still have to choose between like, play all of these, play some of these, practice this one.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:30.700
You know, it's like, I think that there's just something about the nuance of the way they designed it.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:42.160
And to be fair, this game was totally on computers first, but it just feels weird playing this on the NES and made me want like, you know, an Oregon Trail or a number munchers version on the NES.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:43.360
Oh, that would be great.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:45.100
But no, I get that.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:54.440
It's almost like a, a two faithfully done, uh, or taking, taking UI elements from the computer.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:55.680
I, I can see that.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:06.120
And then the second step of that though, which is kind of strange is that when you go into each game, you need a manual, uh, in order to figure out how to play those games.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:14.720
Because if you don't, you know, if you just like go into the level, there's no like assistance of like, you know, using the D pads or using the, the buttons.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:16.300
And so you, you need the manual there.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:21.440
And I know that sounds strange because like technically you always need a manual to find out what you control in a game.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:35.180
But when you're talking about, you know, I think eight or nine different, um, you know, little games inside of one game, it feels like that splash page could have benefited from like a little line on the bottom, just explaining the controls.

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:37.020
They could have had two pages.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:38.780
There's no reason not to.

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:53.400
Um, uh, the fact that, that how to play the game is buried in the, is buried in the manual, like beyond the, like just like, like a lot of man, like a lot of games you want to read the manual just for like broad strokes.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:07:07.760
But this is like, uh, it's like an instruction manual, like an instruction booklet of how to build an Ikea chair and given you like the exact like button inputs that you need to, to actually do the thing that you're supposed to do.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:15.660
A game, a game built out of many games needs to have those instructions in the game experience, in my opinion.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:25.340
Yeah. It's weird that when they were play testing this, that they didn't, there wasn't someone who realized, hey, every time we play a game, we have to reference the manual every time we get to a new thing.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.720
And we also have these splash screens that have information on them.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:31.560
And like, why don't we meld the two?

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:32.740
Yeah, I agree.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:38.380
Yeah. It reminds me of, um, Mario Party, you know, like there's like 70 mini games in there.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:45.040
If they didn't explain before you play that very mini game, if you had to go to the manual, like those board games would take even longer.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:54.720
So yes, it is a little bizarre, but it also doesn't help that all of these games, uh, you know, don't control in the most intuitive sense that you would think of.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:08:02.000
You know, I don't know how you guys felt, but it's a lot of just left and right on the D-pad, uh, in a, in a rhythm fashion.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.520
And I just thought that was like very strange.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:08.680
So I would never guess to do most of the things that they wanted me to do in these games.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:11.780
So I definitely needed to keep going back to the manual anytime I played one of these.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:31.920
I, I'd say that like my experience with this, this game with many of the mini games, not all of them, but, uh, it almost felt like, like, like a rhythm game, like you said, but it was like, if you're playing DDR, but you didn't get to see the inputs like scrolling or like guitar hero without seeing what button you're supposed to press.

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:54.260
You just sort of have to, like, you know, that you only have this many options of what to do, but like the rhythm changes depending on, uh, like whatever sliding scale that you're allowed to adjust, but you just, you, you have to keep, you have to keep doing this just to figure out what the correct, like rhythm of input is.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:01.420
Yeah. I felt that way, particularly like about the weightlifting, maybe just cause it was the first one that I played, but.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:12.740
But that felt very much like, okay, the more weight you add, it like increases or decreases the window in which you have to like, it changes when you have to change your position of your character.

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:14.880
But like, you don't know when that is.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.400
Yeah. You have to do a lot of experimenting to figure that out.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:22.400
There are, there are cues, but you have to, you have to learn them by failing 500 times.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.940
Right. There are cues for some of them.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:30.880
For others, I feel like I, well, I think that there's no feedback.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:34.620
Not, not just cues, but like feedback for when you do something wrong.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:40.220
Oh yeah, for sure. It just, in some of the games, you can't tell if what you're doing is good or bad.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:52.180
Where I, if I'm, you know, rolling in the log and my guy suddenly falls, I'm not sure if it's because the other guy changed direction or if I went too fast or too slow or what, you know?

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:55.580
And I know there's like the meter or anything, but it's not really explained.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:59.120
And don't even get me started on the bull riding too.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:02.100
Oh God. No, I, yeah.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:05.600
There's a couple of games in here that I was just like, all right, no, no chance I'm doing

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:07.980
well in this. And there's other games that I thought were too easy.

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:13.180
It seems like we have to start breaking down the games before we go any further.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.340
Cause otherwise we're just going to accidentally do it.

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:21.180
But I'd like to start with the game that I actually thought translated maybe the best out of all of them.

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:23.800
And that was the weightlifting from Russia.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:26.660
I technically, I think we lift weights all over the world.

00:10:26.900 --> 00:10:29.280
With love. Yeah, sure, Sean.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:32.160
But you know what I mean? Like we lift weights all over the world.

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:34.560
So again, I'm not sure why they chose Russia for weightlifting.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:37.040
It's not like they're doing specific Russian power lifts either.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:44.920
So in this one, you have to like, you're just using the, the up and down deep head buttons,

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:49.000
but you have to time them based on like your lift and your like maximum pull.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:51.420
And if you hold too long, you'll, you'll drop it.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:52.600
I thought that was kind of interesting.

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:57.680
You have to change between like when you should go up versus when you should bend back down and

00:10:57.680 --> 00:10:59.320
you know, uh, all that.

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:05.500
So even though it was only two buttons, I found this one to be very, uh, easy to pull off.

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:07.680
The lower weights are definitely more forgiving.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:12.180
Um, but I actually did eventually clear like the highest weight.

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:18.100
So it's not impossible, but it's fun to like learn the instances of like, okay, I need

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:22.400
to hold for a little bit longer before I lift to the next step because otherwise I won't

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:24.620
have like enough energy and I'll just drop it right away.

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:25.860
You guys, do you guys know what I'm talking about?

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:30.040
I get what you're saying, but this is the, this is the mini game I was referencing where

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:33.820
it just felt like I was playing DDR without the, like the input scrolling.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:40.600
It, it, it really kind of annoyed me that again, like there just wasn't feedback to,

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:43.880
uh, to, to, to show me how I was doing it wrong.

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:49.880
Uh, this is like, this is the one where I'm like, yeah, I, I, I eventually found the, uh,

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:54.780
the cues for when you're supposed to shift to the opposite direction, but I didn't like

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:57.320
this one at all. I think I fall kind of in the middle there.

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:01.080
Cause I, I see what, I definitely saw what you were saying, Mike, while I was playing

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:03.680
it where I was like, well, this is interesting the way they did it.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:08.000
But I did wish that they gave me some indication of like teaching me how to do it or, you know,

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:10.580
like giving other than just trying over and over again and failing.

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:15.920
Um, but yeah, I thought it was, I thought it was, it, it would maybe even be like something

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:19.580
if you, if you've never, if you've never lifted weights before, this would like, Hey, give

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:20.920
you an idea of like what to expect.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.940
Like this is the kind of, it gives you the idea of like how you should do it technique

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.040
or whatever. You know what I mean? It's like, it's like an entry level thing.

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:29.760
Um, so I was like, Oh, that's kind of cool that they translated it like that.

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:33.300
But I did feel like it was, it was definitely lacking in like, I don't know.

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:35.340
So yeah, feedback, I guess.

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:41.560
Yeah. When I got it and when I completed the lift, I felt like, all right, yeah, I get this.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:43.180
Like I understand the motions of it.

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:49.800
I kind of feel the flow of this game, but then sometimes I feel like I do the same thing

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:53.460
and then I just drop it and I wasn't exactly sure why.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:55.900
Um, I don't know.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:59.760
I guess it's the kind of thing where if I play more than I would, I would really hone in

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:06.780
on what exactly to do, but just because there is this lack of feedback, it, it gets frustrating.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:09.540
Right. I see what you're saying there, Sam.

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:10.760
Like ideally, right.

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:15.460
The NES has like a rumble pack built into the controller and you're able to like feel your

00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:16.580
legs shaking.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:20.780
If you, if you like hold in the position too long or something like that visually though

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:24.240
too. No, that's what I was going to say next is like, yeah, they could have totally done that,

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:28.740
you know, where, um, you see the legs quake or you see the arms start to give out or

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:30.760
change a color or something like that.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:32.740
I just, I don't know.

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:37.840
I didn't find it to be that difficult though, because I guess I'm thinking about it, you

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:42.480
know, as, as like the lift, you know, it's like, you can't just jump right into the next

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:47.020
pose when you think you kind of need to like hold where you are for a second, get your footing

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:51.600
and then go. So you have to like almost, you know, you have to have like a beat in your head to it of

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:54.260
like, okay, you know, I I'm in the squat.

00:13:54.500 --> 00:13:58.580
Now I got to like come up a little bit and then I got to switch back over to, you know,

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:00.980
get rested on my, uh, collarbone.

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:02.500
I, you know, I thought that was working.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:04.740
Okay. I mean that, that to each their own.

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:33.800
All right.

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:37.500
And, you know, Rare only published this game.

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:38.780
Epix was the one who made it.

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:45.320
So it's not quite the slalom that Sam is looking for, but Sam, what did you think here of another

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:47.240
variation of slalom on the NES?

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:48.860
Oh, this one sucked, man.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:53.500
This compared to, especially compared to the goat slalom.

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:58.380
Uh, this one I felt it was just, it was just weird.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:04.180
And I don't know about you guys, but I always felt like, like the controls were backwards

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:07.880
sometimes, like not actually backwards, but it like mixed it up in my head.

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:10.120
And I don't know.

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:18.080
I could see how there's like a rhythm and there's physics to this one, but I just, it just didn't

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:22.800
seem that fun to me to act to when to finally get it.

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:25.840
I don't know. I wasn't feeling this one at all.

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:28.180
I much prefer ski free.

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:29.260
Oh, ski free.

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:31.660
Yeah. I thought you said ski three.

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:34.140
And I'm like, oh. That's a different way, right?

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:37.080
Of handling, um, the skiing thing.

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:41.020
It's like this view is, it's a little too tight for what they're trying to do.

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:46.040
They needed to like pull back and spread out the flag poles a little bit, you know, like

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:49.540
I'm sure whatever they were trying to do it, like how it is done in the Olympics or what

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:53.620
have you. But like, it would have just helped the user a lot more to prepare for these turns.

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:57.600
I felt like some of them, I was just like, I just accepted.

00:15:57.700 --> 00:15:58.820
I was like, all right, not making that one.

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:00.760
Try to make the next one. You know, like you just move on.

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:06.540
Yeah. I couldn't miss, I couldn't miss less than seven of those.

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:11.400
Like every time I had to miss at least seven if I wanted to, to make it, you know, without,

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:12.540
without wiping out.

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:20.560
I feel like, yeah, if they, if they zoomed out a bit, uh, made the, uh, made the flags

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:25.800
a bit further away and also sped the game up, we might just, we, I might've been just

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:30.620
as bad at it, but it would have felt a bit more fun just with that added sense of speed.

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:36.560
